<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neustria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Neustrasia, regnum Neustriae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neustria was the western part of the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, first the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the Silva Carbonaria, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> as its main cities. For more than two hundred years it was at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, the eastern Frankish realm, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a> defeated the Neustrians at Tertry in 687. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> the name passed to a smaller kingdom between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and the Loire, and in 861 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">the Marches</a> of Neustria against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> and the Norsemen. The family of Robert I ruled Neustria until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> became king in 987, after which the term fell out of use.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…family inheritance, dividing it among their sons, so that it was repeatedly split into kingdoms that came to be known as Austrasia in the east, <b>Neustria</b> in the west and Burgundy, and reunited when lines died out, as under Chlothar I in 558 and Chlothar II in 613.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A joint Breton-Viking army defeated the West Franks near Brissarthe in <b>Neustria</b>, killing the Neustrian margrave Robert the Strong and Ranulf I, Duke of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dagobert I, king of Austrasia since 623, became ruler of <b>Neustria</b> and Burgundy as well, and so of all the Frankish kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
